course: dictionary
lesson: 7658
title: Leaping words in context
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words describe a movement you can picture. Context helps you see it. «Springboks and some gazelles leap high with stiff legs, called pronking or stotting.» The clues are leap high and stiff legs, so pronking is a jump up into the air with the legs held straight.
explain: The word can also be a noun. «The young lamb made a happy pronk across the field.» A pronk is one leap of this kind, so a lamb can make a pronk, or several pronks, when it is playful.
explain: Check your meaning by swapping it into the sentence. Try «The springbok leaped high with stiff legs» in place of «pronked». If the sentence still makes sense, your meaning is probably right.
fact: Pronk is a verb, and its forms are pronks, pronked and pronking.
fact: Pronk can also be a noun: a pronk is one such leap.
fact: «Pronk» means to leap high into the air with all four legs stiff, often several times.
fact: «Stotting» is another name for pronking, used in nature lessons.
fact: Nature books say that springboks pronk to show predators like cheetahs that they are fit and fast, so the predator may not bother to chase them.
exercise: «Springboks and some gazelles leap high with stiff legs, called pronking or stotting.» What does pronking mean? | *Leaping high with all four legs stiff | Sleeping in the long grass | Running very fast in a straight line | Drinking at a river
exercise: «The young lamb made a happy pronk across the field.» What is a pronk here? | A long drink | *A high leap with stiff legs | A loud bleat | A sleepy yawn
exercise: «Gazelles escape cheetahs by running and dodging, and they sometimes pronk.» Why might a gazelle pronk when a cheetah is near? | *To show it is fit and fast, so it is not worth chasing | To hide in the long grass | To ask the cheetah to play | To wake up the herd for a nap
test: Which word is the past tense of pronk? | pronks | pronking | *pronked | a pronk
test: «Lambs and goats sometimes pronk when playful.» What does this tell you about pronking? | It only happens when an animal is ill | It means the animal is hungry | *It can be a sign of play | It happens only in the dark
exam: A springbok pronks high over the grass as a cheetah watches. What is the springbok most likely trying to show? | That it wants to sleep in the shade | *That it is fit and fast, so not worth chasing | That it is lost and needs help | That it is afraid of the grass
